## Using @llvm.trap

`@llvm.trap` triggers an immediate, non-recoverable program termination.

```xiom
fn div(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int {
  if b == 0 { @llvm.trap(); }
  return a / b;
}
```

### trap vs Contract Violation

| Feature | Contract Violation | @llvm.trap |
|---------|-------------------|------------|
| Message | Detailed | None |
| Overhead | Condition eval + formatting | Minimal |
| Strippable | Yes (--no-contracts) | No |
| Use case | Development/debugging | Production safety |

### When to Use trap
```xiom
fn unreachable_branch() -> Int {
  match some_enum {
    VariantA => 1,
    VariantB => 2,
    _ => { @llvm.trap(); }  // Should never happen
  }
}
```

### Adding Messages with trap
```xiom
fn connect(host: Str, port: Int) {
  if port <= 0 || port > 65535 {
    io.eprintln("Invalid port: " + port.to_str());
    @llvm.trap();
  }
}
```
Print the message before trapping — @llvm.trap() takes no arguments.

